    "case_id": "414",
    "case_summary": "A 32-year-old African American female. At age 13, she developed elevated intraocular pressure. She had taller stature than her relatives (180.4 cm), long fingers, and hypermobility of the shoulders, digits, and knees. Family history was negative for similar findings. Serial echocardiograms showed no abnormalities.\n\n- Open iridocorneal angle in each eye- No evidence of Haab striae, buphthalmos, or high iris insertion- Subtle lens dislocation superonasally in the right eye (first observed at age 17)- Later developed lens dislocation in the left eye (at age 32)- Hypermobility of shoulders, digits, and knees\n\n- Genetic Testing:  Comprehensive connective tissue genetic testing panel including 92 genes (Invitae) revealed 2 heterozygous variants in the LTBP2 gene (c.709C>T p.Arg237* and c.3776-1G>C splice acceptor)- Imaging:  Figure 1A and B: Fundus photographs showing substantial optic nerve cupping and loss of neural rim tissue at age 17 years  Figure 1C and D: Slitlamp photographs demonstrating progressive superonasal dislocation of the native lens over more than a decade",
    "frontier": [
      "B1",
      "B2",
      "B3"
    ],
    "root": {
      "alarm_features": [
        "Progressive optic nerve cupping and loss of neural rim tissue"
